    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(6))  # F0..F5\n    customers = list(range(12))  # C0..C11\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(91, 10), (98, 78), (29, 50), (33, 60), (87, 1), (75, 83)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(29, 62), (78, 86), (5, 68), (74, 62), (7, 23), (51, 43), (73, 29), (5, 20), (16, 85), (66, 3), (35, 52), (47, 66)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [163.0, 130.0, 195.0, 64.0, 141.0, 61.0]\n    customer_demands = [20.0, 11.0, 26.0, 18.0, 22.0, 14.0, 38.0, 23.0, 21.0, 33.0, 27.0, 15.0]\n    fixed_costs = [235.0, 412.0, 266.0, 231.0, 135.0, 461.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse network decision \u2014 selecting sites and allocating customer demand\n\nWe\u2019re at a point where we need to choose which warehouses to open and how best to ship product to our customers. We have six candidate warehouse locations across our service region, with their positions laid out in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (91, 10)\n  - F1: (98, 78)\n  - F2: (29, 50)\n  - F3: (33, 60)\n  - F4: (87, 1)\n  - F5: (75, 83). Our customers are spread across twelve delivery points, and their map coordinates are listed in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (29, 62)\n  - C1: (78, 86)\n  - C2: (5, 68)\n  - C3: (74, 62)\n  - C4: (7, 23)\n  - C5: (51, 43)\n  - C6: (73, 29)\n  - C7: (5, 20)\n  - C8: (16, 85)\n  - C9: (66, 3)\n  - C10: (35, 52)\n  - C11: (47, 66).\n\nFor planning and costing, distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances using these map coordinates. In other words, we measure the crow\u2011flies distance from each warehouse candidate to each customer location for all shipping calculations.\n\nOur cost structure has two parts. First, opening a warehouse triggers a one\u2011time fixed cost; those amounts are provided in [235.0, 412.0, 266.0, 231.0, 135.0, 461.0]. Second, every unit we ship incurs a variable delivery cost based on how far it travels and how much we send, using a transport rate of 0.5.\n\nOperationally, each warehouse can only handle a limited amount of volume, and those maximum throughputs are specified in [163.0, 130.0, 195.0, 64.0, 141.0, 61.0]. On the demand side, every customer must be fully supplied; required volumes are detailed in [20.0, 11.0, 26.0, 18.0, 22.0, 14.0, 38.0, 23.0, 21.0, 33.0, 27.0, 15.0]. We can only ship from sites we choose to open, and we must respect the capacity limits at those sites.\n\nThe decisions in front of us are straightforward to state but interconnected: which subset of warehouse locations to open, and how to assign each customer\u2019s orders to the open sites so that service is reliable and costs are kept in check. Our goal is to minimize total cost\u2014combining the fixed expense of opening facilities with the variable shipping costs\u2014while fully meeting all customer demand and staying within each facility\u2019s capacity.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse network decision \u2014 selecting sites and allocating customer demand\n\nWe\u2019re at a point where we need to choose which warehouses to open and how best to ship product to our customers. We have six candidate warehouse locations across our service region, with their positions laid out in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}. Our customers are spread across twelve delivery points, and their map coordinates are listed in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}.\n\nFor planning and costing, distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances using these map coordinates. In other words, we measure the crow\u2011flies distance from each warehouse candidate to each customer location for all shipping calculations.\n\nOur cost structure has two parts. First, opening a warehouse triggers a one\u2011time fixed cost; those amounts are provided in {FIXED_COSTS}. Second, every unit we ship incurs a variable delivery cost based on how far it travels and how much we send, using a transport rate of {TRANSPORT_RATE}.\n\nOperationally, each warehouse can only handle a limited amount of volume, and those maximum throughputs are specified in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}. On the demand side, every customer must be fully supplied; required volumes are detailed in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. We can only ship from sites we choose to open, and we must respect the capacity limits at those sites.\n\nThe decisions in front of us are straightforward to state but interconnected: which subset of warehouse locations to open, and how to assign each customer\u2019s orders to the open sites so that service is reliable and costs are kept in check. Our goal is to minimize total cost\u2014combining the fixed expense of opening facilities with the variable shipping costs\u2014while fully meeting all customer demand and staying within each facility\u2019s capacity."
